<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Workers' and Peasants' Red Army</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red Army was the army of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in 1918 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. It conquered the independent republics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1920–1921 and later drove the German armies back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It was renamed the Soviet Army in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-army.jpg" alt="Red Army" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RKKA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soviet_Red_Army_Hammer_and_Sickle.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Workers' and Peasants' Red Army was the army and air force of the Russian Soviet Republic and, from 1922, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It was established in January 1918 by a decree of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> to oppose the forces of the new state's adversaries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>, above all the groups known together as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Army</a>. In September 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> had written that the standing army should be replaced by the arming of the entire people. The Imperial Russian Army was by then collapsing: the Tsarist general Nikolay Dukhonin estimated 2 million deserters, 1.8 million dead, 5 million wounded and 2 million prisoners.</p><p>At first the Red Army was a voluntary formation, without ranks or insignia, and its officers were chosen by democratic election. A decree of 29 May 1918 imposed compulsory military service for men aged 18 to 40.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1919 the White armies advanced from the south under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a>, from the east under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Aleksandr Kolchak</a> and from the northwest under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yudenich">Nikolai Yudenich</a>, and at first beat the Red Army back on each front. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> reformed it and counterattacked: Kolchak's army was repelled in June and those of Denikin and Yudenich in October. The Red Army also invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the Polish-Soviet War, reaching the centre of the country in 1920 before a decisive defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> ended the war.</p><p>In the 1930s the army fought border conflicts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, culminating in the victory at Khalkhin Gol in September 1939. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, which began on 30 November 1939, it had three times as many soldiers as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> and a hundred times as many tanks, but its performance was hindered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> of 1937, in which more than 30,000 of its officers were executed or imprisoned. Finland held out much longer than the Soviets expected.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, which the Soviets called the Great Patriotic War, the Red Army provided the largest ground force in the Allied victory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and fought the largest and deadliest land war in history. Up to 34 million soldiers served in it. Its performance in the opening stages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> was mixed and often poor, but it ultimately broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> front and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Its personnel were also involved in Soviet war crimes against civilians and prisoners of war, particularly in Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. Officially it lost 8,668,300 killed, missing or dead of disease or in captivity, though other estimates run to almost 11 million.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In February 1946 the Red Army was renamed the Soviet Army. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> it was split between the post-Soviet states, and its bulk became the Russian Ground Forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Red Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Republic of Armenia, declared on 28 May 1918, was invaded in 1920 by Turkish nationalist forces and by the 11th <b>Red Army</b>, which entered Yerevan in early December; the Treaty of Kars then ceded Kars, Ardahan and Surmalu, with Mount Ararat, to Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Red Army</b> took Azerbaijan in April 1920, the Armenian Bolsheviks led an uprising at Alexandropol in May, which the government suppressed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It lasted 23 months: the Bolshevik 11th <b>Red Army</b> invaded in April 1920, as many as 20,000 Azerbaijani soldiers died resisting, and the Azerbaijan Soviet Socialist Republic followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Czechoslovak airmen flew with the Royal Air Force, a battalion fought at Tobruk, and the 1st Czechoslovak Army Corps under Ludvík Svoboda fought alongside the <b>Red Army</b>, including at the Dukla Pass in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Russian Revolution the Democratic Republic of Georgia declared independence on 26 May 1918 under the Menshevik Noe Zhordania, one of the first states in Europe to give women the vote; the <b>Red Army</b> entered Tbilisi on 25 February 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By early 1945 the army withdrew towards Zagreb with German and Cossack troops, and the advance of the Partisans and the Soviet <b>Red Army</b> drove a mass retreat towards Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
